          "url": "https://gearstrings.com/gear-reviews/wampler-pedals-super-plextortion-and-pinnacle-pedal-review",
          "host": "gearstrings.com",
          "title": "Wampler Pedals Super Plextortion and Pinnacle Review: Dual High-Gain Powerhouses Tested Side-by-Side | GearStrings",
          "h1": "Wampler Pedals Super Plextortion and Pinnacle Review: Dual High-Gain Powerhouses Tested Side-by-Side",
          "description": "A detailed, hands-on comparison of Wampler’s Super Plextortion and Pinnacle overdrive/distortion pedals — covering circuit design, tonal character, gain staging, noise floor, build quality, and real-world performance with Stratocasters, Les Pauls, and various amps including Marshall JCM800, Fender Twin Reverb, and Friedman BE-100.",
          "snippet": "Wampler Pedals Super Plextortion and Pinnacle Review: Dual High-Gain ...",
          "bodyExcerpt": "Wampler Pedals Super Plextortion and Pinnacle Review: Dual High-Gain Powerhouses Tested Side-by-Side | GearStrings GEARSTRINGS Home Blog Categories Authors About Home / Blog / gear reviews gear reviews Wampler Pedals Super Plextortion and Pinnacle Review: Dual High-Gain Powerhouses Tested Side-by-Side By Nina Harper July 10, 2026 Introduction: Two Distinct Paths to High-Gain Tone Wampler Pedals’ Super Plextortion and Pinnacle represent two philosophically divergent approaches to high-gain overdrive within the same product family. The Super Plextortion ($279 USD) is a meticulously voiced recreation of the legendary Plexi-era Marshall tones — specifically targeting the saturated midrange growl of a cranked ’68 Marshall Super Lead 100W head through a 4x12 cabinet loaded with Celestion G12M ‘Greenbacks’. The Pinnacle ($299 USD), by contrast, is a modern high-headroom distortion designed for tight low-end response, articulate note separation, and dynamic sensitivity — engineered to complement high-gain metal and progressive rock rigs without sacrificing touch responsiveness. Both are true-bypass, analog circuits housed in CNC-machined aluminum enclosures measuring 4.75\" × 3.75\" × 2.0\", weighing 1.2 lbs each, and powered by standard 9V DC (center-negative) with a current draw of 22mA (Super Plextortion) and 24mA (Pinnacle). Over six weeks of daily testing across three guitar/amp combinations — a 1963 Fender Stratocaster into a 1978 Marshall JCM800 2203, a 2015 Gibson Les Paul Standard through a Friedman BE-100, and a PRS SE Custom 24 into a Fender Twin Reverb — these pedals revealed nuanced differences in harmonic complexity, compression behavior, and pedalboard integration. Circuit Architecture & Design Philosophy Under the hood, both pedals employ discrete transistor-based gain stages rather than op-amp ICs, preserving organic clipping characteristics and minimizing phase inversion artifacts. The Super Plextortion uses a three-stage JFET-driven topology inspired by the cascading preamp section of a non-master-volume Marshall. Its first stage features a dual-ganged J201 JFET configured for soft asymmetrical clipping; the second stage adds a 2N5457 JFET with adjustable bias via the ‘Tone’ pot; and the third stage employs a matched pair of 2N5459s for output saturation. This architecture deliberately emulates the voltage sag and natural compression of tube-driven signal paths — a key reason why it responds so authentically to guitar volume roll-off (tested from 10 to 3.5 on a 500kΩ CTS pot). Super Plextortion: The Analog Emulation Approach The Super Plextortion’s signal path includes a passive tone stack derived directly from Marshall’s original Baxandall-style EQ network, implemented with 1% metal-film resistors and polypropylene film capacitors (including a 0.022µF Orange Drop coupling cap between stages). Its ‘Gain’ control sweeps from clean boost (at 1–2 o’clock) through classic Plexi crunch (3–5) to full-blown lead saturation (7–10). Crucially, the ‘Volume’ knob is post-clipping but pre-EQ, meaning tone shaping remains consistent regardless of output level — a design choice that preserves midrange focus even at unity gain settings. Pinnacle: The Modern High-Fidelity Engine The Pinnacle departs significantly from vintage emulation. Its four-stage gain structure combines JFETs and silicon diodes in a hybrid clipping configuration: Stages one and two use matched 2SK372 JFETs for low-noise pre-saturation; stage three introduces symmetrical silicon clipping (1N4148 diodes) for aggressive harmonics; and stage four utilizes a proprietary buffered output stage with active EQ feedback. Unlike most high-gain pedals, the Pinnacle’s ‘Drive’ control doesn’t simply increase gain — it dynamically adjusts clipping threshold *and* low-end tightness via an internal 18V virtual rail circuit (achievable with the included Wampler Triple Tap power supply or any isolated 18V DC source). At 9V operation, maximum drive is capped at ~85% of its 18V poten",
