          "bodyExcerpt": "Way Huge Pork Loin Overdrive: The Two-Amp Trick Box Skip to content Dirty Guitar Facts. Loud Legends. R ock G uitars S tories P eople F orever Search GuitarGangsters &#8981; Menu Home Guitar Legends Gear Rigs Guitars Amps Pedals &#038; FX Pickups Accessories DIY Builds Custom Builds Electronics &#038; Wiring Finishing &#038; Relics Mods &#038; Upgrades Rock History 70’s Rock 80’s Metal 90’s Grunge &#038; Alternative 2000’s Revival News Culture Videos About Contact In this guide Housekeeping: this one is discontinued, and that matters Where the Pork Loin came from: the 2008 Way Huge relaunch What “Soft Clip Injection” actually means Full specifications Every control, and what it actually does Famous players — and an unusually honest roster Tone recipes and settings How it compares Buying guide Living with one Frequently asked questions In this guide Housekeeping: this one is discontinued, and that matters Where the Pork Loin came from: the 2008 Way Huge relaunch What “Soft Clip Injection” actually means Full specifications Every control, and what it actually does Famous players — and an unusually honest roster Tone recipes and settings How it compares Buying guide Living with one Frequently asked questions Share this article Home › Gear Rigs › Way Huge Pork Loin Overdrive: The Two-Amp Trick in a Purple Box Gear Rigs Way Huge Pork Loin Overdrive: The Two-Amp Trick in a Purple Box Way Huge Pork Loin Overdrive guide: soft clip injection explained, all four internal trimpots, verified players, tone recipes and used-market buying advice. admin 22 September 2026 25 min read Updated 24 September 2026 GG-ARTICLE-TOP There is a sound that blues and classic-rock players have been chasing since roughly 1968, and almost nobody talks about it honestly. It is not a distortion sound. It is the sound of two amplifiers running at once — one glassy and clean, one cooking — with a Y-cable or an A/B/Y box splitting the guitar between them. Stevie Ray Vaughan built a career on it. So did half of Muscle Shoals. The clean amp keeps the pick attack, the string separation and the top-end sparkle; the dirty amp supplies the weight and the sustain underneath. Neither amp on its own sounds like the record. Together they sound three-dimensional, in a way that a single overdrive pedal into a single amp almost never does. GG-ARTICLE-AFTER-INTRO The problem is obvious: two amps means two amps. Two of everything to carry, two of everything to mic, two of everything to break down at the load-out. Which is why, in late 2008, when Jeorge Tripps was handed the keys to a resurrected Way Huge Electronics by Jim Dunlop, one of the very first things he built was a pedal that does the whole trick internally. He called it the Pork Loin Soft Clip Injection Overdrive , gave it a model number of WHE201 , painted it purple, and — with a straight face — printed the phrase “the masses of pork power that exude from its space age circuitry” in the official product copy. The joke name has cost this pedal a lot of respect over the years. Underneath it is one of the most quietly intelligent overdrive designs of the modern era, and one of the very few that is genuinely useful to guitarists and bassists. It is also, as of now, out of production — which changes the whole conversation about whether you should buy one. We will get to that. How to Get the Tone ↓ Housekeeping: this one is discontinued, and that matters Before anything else, a piece of admin that changes how you should read the rest of this article. The WHE201 Pork Loin is no longer in production. Dunlop’s own retail channel has carried it at $169.99, but the major retail listings now read “no longer available,” and the used market has responded exactly the way used markets respond to a discontinued cult item: as of September 2026, aggregated pricing puts the Pork Loin’s average at roughly $363 , with live listings starting around $266.70 and the category placement bumped up into the “High-end / 